/* ============================================================================
   6g · THE RELATIVITY WING
   Six groups, in the order the ideas actually arrived: the thought experiments
   first, then the geometry they force, then what that does to mechanics, then
   the electromagnetic problem that started it, then curvature, then the waves.
   ============================================================================ */

const REL_GROUPS = [
{ g:"Einstein's thought experiments", items:[
  {n:'Chasing a light beam', ex:'the question he asked at sixteen', stage:'rlChase',
   opts:{beta:0.6},
   out:'Two panes, one wave. Run after it as fast as you like and it still passes you at c — what changes is the colour, by the Doppler factor k = √((1+β)/(1−β)), and the amplitude, by 1/k. The crest markers advance at the same rate in both panes because they must.',
   note:'Einstein said he wondered, at sixteen, what a light wave would look like if he rode alongside it. Maxwell has no answer: a <b>standing</b> electromagnetic wave is not a solution of his equations, and yet Galilean relativity says such a frame must exist. The readout shows the modern resolution as an invariant rather than a velocity. A frozen wave would be a static field, needing <b>B = 0</b> and therefore <b>E² − c²B² ≠ 0</b> — but that combination is the <i>same number in every inertial frame</i>, and for light it is exactly zero. No boost can change it, so no observer can ever catch light. The puzzle is not solved by going fast enough; it is dissolved by noticing that "fast enough" was never a coherent idea.'},
  {n:'Chasing anything at all — and the rate that is not a velocity', ex:'light recedes at c however hard you chase', stage:'rlChase',
   opts:{chmode:'rate', beta:0.9},
   out:'Two numbers, both correct, answering different questions. The <b>coordinate gap</b> between you and the signal shrinks at β_s − β, which for light is 1 − β and can be made as small as you like. What <b>you</b> measure is (β_s − β)/(1 − β_s β), and for light that is a flat line at exactly 1.',
   note:'Set the signal to <b>1</b> and the solid curve is dead flat at 1 across every chase speed there is — that is the second postulate, drawn rather than asserted. Then set it to <b>0.9</b> and watch the two curves separate: a signal slower than light behaves the way intuition expects at low speed and stops doing so at high speed. The number worth staring at is the dashed one where it exceeds c. Set your speed to −0.9 and the signal to 0.9 and the coordinate gap closes at <b>1.8</b>. Nothing is wrong: a closing rate is the derivative of a difference of two positions measured in one frame, and there is no object whose worldline has that slope. Relativity bounds the speed of a <i>thing</i>, and this is not one — which is also why the "spot of light sweeping across the Moon" and the phase velocity of a wave may both exceed c without carrying anything.'},
  {n:'The train and the embankment — simultaneity dies first', ex:"Δt′ = βL₀/c, computed both ways", stage:'rlTrain',
   opts:{beta:0.6},
   out:'Lightning strikes both ends of the train. The embankment says the two strikes were simultaneous. The train says the front was struck first, by exactly βL₀/c. Both are right, and both agree on every arrival.',
   note:'This is the load-bearing idea of the whole subject, and everything else — time dilation, length contraction, the twin paradox, E = mc² — is downstream of it. Two events at the same <b>t</b> but different <b>x</b> get different <b>t′</b>, because <b>t′ = γ(t − βx/c²)</b>. It is not that simultaneity is hard to <i>establish</i> across a distance; it is that different frames pick out different sets of event pairs as simultaneous, and there is no fact of the matter deciding between them. Notice what the two panes never disagree about: which flash reached which observer first. Causal order is safe. Only the order of events that <i>cannot</i> influence one another is up for grabs — and that is exactly the loophole relativity needs.'},
  {n:'Two events you place, and whether anyone can see them in the other order', ex:'the order flips exactly when the pair is spacelike', stage:'rlTrain',
   opts:{trmode:'pair', ekey:'near'},
   out:'The plot is <b>Δt′ = γ(Δt − βΔx)</b> against β. Where it crosses zero the two events are simultaneous for that observer, and either side of the crossing they happen in opposite orders. A crossing exists inside (−1, 1) <b>only</b> if the pair is spacelike.',
   note:'This is causality, and the argument is one line: β = Δt/Δx is a legal boost exactly when |Δx| > |Δt|, which is exactly the definition of spacelike. They are not two conditions that happen to coincide — they are the same condition written twice. Try the <b>almost light</b> preset, which is spacelike by one per cent and needs an observer at 0.99c to reverse it, and then the <b>just timelike</b> one, which is the same pair with Δt nudged from 0.99 to 1.01: the crossover does not creep past 1, it stops existing. That is how sharp the light cone is as a boundary. And notice what never moves whatever the order does: <b>s²</b>. Two observers can disagree about which of two events came first, and they cannot disagree about whether a signal could have connected them — so nothing that could be influenced is ever reordered, and relativity gets to take simultaneity away without taking causality with it.'},
  {n:'The light clock — time dilation from Pythagoras', ex:'(ct/2)² = (vt/2)² + h²', stage:'rlClock',
   opts:{beta:0.7},
   out:'Two identical clocks, one carried past at β. The moving photon must travel the hypotenuse at the same c, so its tick takes γ times longer. Watch the two tick counters separate, and check the right triangle closing in the readout — the residual is zero to machine precision.',
   note:'The cleanest argument in physics: one postulate and one theorem from school geometry. Nothing about the clock\'s construction enters, which is the point — if a light clock and a wristwatch disagreed, you could tell which frame you were in by comparing them, and the first postulate forbids that. So it is not a fact about clocks. It is a fact about time. Rearranged, <b>t²(1 − β²) = t₀²</b>, and the invariant hiding inside is the spacetime interval: <b>(ct)² − x² = (ct₀)²</b>. Every clock in the moving frame — mechanical, atomic, biological, radioactive — runs slow by the same factor, and cosmic-ray muons in the "Energy & momentum" stage are the measurement.'},
  {n:'Point the mirror anywhere — the clock does not care', ex:'legs (1+β)/(1−β) apart, and a tick of exactly γ', stage:'rlClock',
   opts:{clmode:'place', ckey:'along', beta:0.6},
   out:'The textbook light clock points its mirror straight across the motion, because that is the case Pythagoras does in one line. This one points it straight <b>ahead</b>: the light chases a mirror running away, then meets an emitter that has moved on, and the two halves of the tick are in the ratio 4 at β = 0.6. The total is still exactly γ times the rest tick.',
   note:'Michelson and Morley built an interferometer to compare exactly these two arms, expecting the along-the-motion one to disagree with the across-the-motion one as the Earth swung through the ether. The panel shows why it does not — and the ingredient that makes it work is <b>length contraction</b>: the arm along the motion is shorter by γ in the lab, and the readout prints the contracted mirror position beside the rest one. Take that division out and the two arms disagree by 25% at β = 0.6, which is the fringe shift they went looking for. (The engine here had that bug on its first run, and the unit tests caught it — the along-the-motion clock ticked 3.125 against the across-the-motion clock\'s 2.5.) Put the mirror at 45°, or at a shallow angle, or a long way off: the legs come apart differently every time and the total never notices. That is what "no experiment can detect uniform motion" actually means, and it is why it is not a fact about clocks.'},
  {n:'The ladder in the barn — a paradox that is not one', ex:'both doors shut, in one frame only', stage:'rlBarn',
   opts:{beta:0.8},
   out:'The barn frame sees a contracted ladder that fits, and shuts both doors together. The ladder frame sees a contracted barn it cannot possibly fit in — and sees the far door shut and reopen before the near one shuts at all. Nothing is ever hit.',
   note:'Length contraction is reciprocal, and that only sounds contradictory until you notice what "the length of a moving object" means: the distance between where its two ends are <b>at the same time</b> — and the two frames use different sets of "at the same time". The two door-closings are spacelike separated, so their order is frame-dependent by the previous stage, and the statement "the ladder was entirely inside with both doors shut" is not a fact about the world at all. It is a fact about a frame. The invariant facts — nothing collided, the ladder came out the far end intact — are agreed by everyone, as they must be.'},
  {n:'The same paradox as four events, with your own numbers', ex:'the two closings are reorderable only if they are spacelike', stage:'rlBarn',
   opts:{bmode:'events', bkey:'classic'},
   out:'Stop arguing about lengths and write down the four <b>events</b> — each end of the ladder passing each door. The panel lists them in the barn frame, transforms them into the ladder frame, and computes the gap between the two door-closings a third time from the ladder\'s own geometry with no boost in it at all.',
   note:'The resolution turns on one question: <b>can a signal get from one door-closing to the other?</b> If not, their order is not a fact about the world and the two frames are free to disagree. The panel draws the light cone from the near-door closing so you can see whether the far one is inside it — and here is the thing the textbook version hides: <b>whether it is depends on your numbers</b>. The paradox needs L/γ > B(1−β). Try <b>roomy</b>, a short ladder in a big barn, where the two closings are <i>timelike</i> separated and every frame agrees which shut first — no paradox, same physics. Then try <b>on the cone</b>, where L/γ = B(1−β) exactly and s² comes out zero to the last bit. This module asserted "the doors are always spacelike" in its first draft and the unit tests found that preset immediately.'},
  {n:'The elevator — his happiest thought', ex:'a falling man feels no gravity', stage:'rlElevator',
   opts:{mode:'accel', exag:12},
   out:'Four boxes. In two of them a ball drops and a light beam bends; in the other two the ball floats and the beam is straight. From inside, the accelerating box and the box at rest on Earth are indistinguishable — and so are the falling box and the one in deep space.',
   note:'In 1907 Einstein realised that a man falling off a roof would not feel his own weight, and called it the happiest thought of his life. Promote it to a principle — <b>gravitation and acceleration are locally the same thing</b> — and two predictions follow immediately from special relativity alone, with no field equations needed. First, light must bend in a gravitational field, because it visibly bends in the accelerating box (the floor rises to meet it). Second, clocks lower down must run slow, by <b>gh/c²</b>, because the receiver in the accelerating box is running away from the light by the time it arrives. Pound and Rebka measured the second up a 22.5 m tower at Harvard in 1959; Eddington measured the first at the solar limb forty years earlier. Both numbers are in the readout, computed rather than quoted.'},
  {n:'Measure both of the elevator\'s predictions', ex:'the bend and the clock shift, computed two ways each', stage:'rlElevator',
   opts:{emode:'measure', gkey:'strong'},
   out:'Two predictions follow from the happiest thought alone, with no field equations anywhere. Light <b>bends</b>, because the floor rises under it — integrated step by step in the accelerating box, and taken from ½gw² in the field. And clocks at different heights <b>disagree</b>, because the receiver is running away by the time the signal arrives.',
   note:'The clock shift is the one worth looking at closely. The familiar <b>gh/c²</b> is not the answer — it is the <i>first term</i> of the exact relativistic Doppler shift of a receiver that has gained speed gh, and the panel computes both so the difference has somewhere to appear. It is about gh/2 of the shift itself, which at Pound and Rebka\'s 22.5 m tower is a correction to a correction of 2×10⁻¹⁵ and utterly invisible, and at the accelerations here is not. Einstein got both predictions in 1907 from this box, eight years before he had the field equations to derive them properly — and both were confirmed: the bend by Eddington at the solar limb in 1919, the shift by Pound and Rebka at Harvard in 1959.'},
  {n:'The rotating disk — where flat geometry breaks', ex:'C / 2πr = γ, with no gravity anywhere', stage:'rlDisk',
   opts:{brim:0.6},
   out:'Rulers laid round the rim are moving along their own length, so each contracts and you need γ times as many. Rulers laid along a radius are moving sideways and do not contract at all. The disk therefore measures C = 2πRγ against a radius R — and C/2πR > 1.',
   note:'Ehrenfest posed this in 1909 and it is the hinge of the whole story. There is no gravity here, no mass, nothing but a spinning disk — and its own measurements are already non-Euclidean. Combine that with the elevator: if acceleration alone bends geometry, and acceleration is locally indistinguishable from gravity, then <b>gravity is bent geometry</b>. This is the argument that sent Einstein to Marcel Grossmann in 1912 to learn Riemannian geometry, and it cost him three more years. Look also at the clock rate on the rim, <b>√(1 − ω²r²/c²)</b>, and compare it with the Schwarzschild factor <b>√(1 − rs/r)</b> in the curved-spacetime group: both are <b>√(1 + 2Φ/c²)</b> for their respective potentials. The disk was a working model of gravitational time dilation six years before anyone had the field equations.'},
  {n:'Survey the disk yourself — and count the rulers', ex:'C/2R = πγ, by closed form and by counting', stage:'rlDisk',
   opts:{dmode:'survey', dkey:'fast'},
   out:'Set the radius, the spin and the length of your ruler. The panel computes C/2R two ways: the closed form <b>πγ</b>, and a <b>count</b> — how many contracted rulers it takes to get round the rim, which is what a surveyor standing on it would actually do.',
   note:'The two routes converge as the rulers shrink, and they must: you cannot lay down a fraction of a ruler, so the last one is always rounded up and a coarse survey always overshoots. The number to stare at is the <b>excess over π</b>, which is quadratic in the rim speed — πv²/2 — so a bicycle wheel is non-Euclidean in the sixteenth decimal place and a rim at half c is out by fifteen per cent. That is why nobody noticed until Ehrenfest raised it in 1909, and why it mattered so much when someone did: there is no gravity here, no mass, nothing but a spinning disk, and its own measurements are already not Euclid\'s. Put that beside the elevator — where acceleration and gravity are locally the same thing — and the conclusion is forced: <b>gravity is bent geometry</b>. That chain of reasoning is what sent Einstein to Marcel Grossmann in 1912 to learn Riemannian geometry, and it cost him three more years.'},
]},
{ g:'The geometry of spacetime', items:[
  {n:'The Minkowski diagram — a boost is a rotation', ex:'drag the event; s² never moves', stage:'rlMink',
   opts:{beta:0.5, ex:1.4, et:2.0},
   out:'Two sets of axes on one diagram. Slide β and the primed axes scissor towards the light cone rather than turning rigidly — and the calibration hyperbolae show where each frame\'s unit tick and unit ruler land. Click anywhere to move the event and watch (ct, x) change while (ct)² − x² does not.',
   note:'"Henceforth space by itself, and time by itself, are doomed to fade away into mere shadows" — Minkowski, 1908, three years after Einstein and rather to Einstein\'s initial annoyance. The reframing is what made general relativity possible: once a boost is a <b>rotation</b>, the question "what else can spacetime do?" has an obvious answer, and the answer is curve. The hyperbolic angle is the <b>rapidity</b> <b>φ = artanh β</b>, with <b>γ = cosh φ</b> and <b>βγ = sinh φ</b> — the same relations an ordinary rotation has with cos and sin, and the reason the invariant is a difference of squares rather than a sum. Note which events lie inside the cone and which outside, and which class has a frame-independent time order.'},
  {n:'A worldline you write, and the clock that follows it', ex:'τ measured in two frames; nothing beats the straight route', stage:'rlMink',
   opts:{mode:'world', wkey:'shuttle', beta:0.6},
   out:'Two pictures of one worldline: the lab\'s, and the same events replotted in the coordinates of an observer moving at β. The proper time along it is integrated twice — once by each — and the panel prints the difference with its scale. Type your own x(t) and the machinery does not change.',
   note:'This is the event mode\'s statement upgraded from a point to a curve. A point has an interval; a curve has a <b>length</b>, and that length is the wristwatch time of whoever follows it. The second route is deliberately not the first one rearranged: rewriting the primed integral back in t makes the γ factors cancel algebraically, and a test that cannot fail is not a test — so it <b>inverts</b> t′(t) numerically, never touches the analytic derivative, and integrates on its own grid. Watch the dashed grey polygon: in Euclidean geometry an inscribed polygon is <i>shorter</i> than its curve, and here it is <b>longer</b>, because every chord is the straight route between two events and the straight route is the longest one. That single reversed inequality is the whole twin paradox, and the dotted line is the twin who stayed put.'},
  {n:'Adding velocities — and the quantity that really adds', ex:'0.75c + 0.75c = 0.96c', stage:'rlVel',
   opts:{u:0.75, v:0.75, ckey:'classic'},
   out:'Galileo\'s dashed line walks straight through the light barrier without noticing it. The actual sum, (u+v)/(1+uv/c²), curls over and touches c only in the limit. Below, your chain of boosts: the speed saturating against c and the rapidity — rescaled into the same box — going up as a straight line.',
   note:'The awkward composition rule is nothing but <b>tanh(φ₁ + φ₂)</b> written in terms of tanh φ. That is worth internalising, because it explains why c is unreachable in a way that "the formula says so" does not: reaching c means reaching infinite rapidity, and no finite number of finite boosts gets there. Write your own chain in the sheet — one β per line, <b>×N</b> to repeat one — and it is composed three ways that share no arithmetic: folding the velocity rule, adding the rapidities, and multiplying the 2×2 Lorentz matrices. The third knows neither formula, and it also certifies that the product is still a boost. Feed the rule a photon and it returns c unchanged, from any frame, which is the postulate it was reverse-engineered to satisfy.'},
  {n:'A chain of boosts, and where the arithmetic runs out', ex:'twenty boosts of 0.9c: 1 − β = 5×10⁻²⁶', stage:'rlVel',
   opts:{u:0.9, v:0.9, ckey:'saturate'},
   out:'Twenty boosts of 0.9c. The rapidity reaches 29.4 and keeps every digit; the speed reaches exactly 1.0 in float64 at boost 14 and has nothing left to say. The panel names the step where that happened rather than printing a residual it cannot support.',
   note:'1 − tanh φ ≈ 2e<sup>−2φ</sup>, so past a total rapidity of about 19 there is no double between the composed speed and c. This is not a bug to be guarded away — it is the reason nobody quotes a beam speed. The LHC\'s protons are always described by their energy, 6.8 TeV, or equivalently by γ ≈ 7 250 or a rapidity of 9.6; "0.999999991c" is a number you can write down and not one you can compute with. Try the <b>there and back</b> preset too: a boost and its inverse compose to <i>exactly</i> zero, and a residual of zero against a scale of zero means nothing at all, so the panel prints it against the gross rapidity that cancelled. And try <b>a ladder of ten</b>: collinear boosts commute, so shuffling the ten changes nothing — a fact that is <i>false</i> in 3+1 for boosts along different axes, where the discrepancy is the Wigner rotation behind Thomas precession.'},
  {n:'The twin paradox — and the ledger that settles it', ex:'proper time is the length of a worldline', stage:'rlTwin',
   opts:{beta:0.8, T:5},
   out:'Both twins travel between the same two events, and each ages by the length of their own worldline. The straight route is the <i>longest</i> in proper time — which is the one thing Minkowski geometry does differently from Euclid. The signal ledger balances exactly: every flash sent is a flash received.',
   note:'The paradox is supposed to be the symmetry — each twin sees the other\'s clock running slow — and the resolution is that the symmetry is not there. Only one twin changes frames, and the simultaneity lines show what that costs: at the turnaround, the traveller\'s notion of "now on Earth" jumps forward by <b>2β²T</b>, sweeping across the shaded wedge of Earth history in an instant. Nothing happens to Earth; what changes is a definition. The Doppler ledger avoids the whole argument by counting only what is <i>seen</i>: the traveller watches Earth redshifted then blueshifted for equal halves of their own trip, while Earth watches the traveller redshifted for far longer than blueshifted, because the news of the turn takes years to arrive. Same k-factor, unequal shares — and that asymmetry is observable, not conventional.'},
  {n:'Programme the engine, and let the geometry do the rest', ex:'dφ/dτ = a(τ) — rapidity is what an engine delivers', stage:'rlTwin',
   opts:{tmode:'prog', twmkey:'turn'},
   out:'The turnaround as an <b>acceleration</b> rather than a kink: four legs of two years at one g, and the traveller comes home to where they started. Each worldline carries a dot per year of its <i>own</i> clock — count them. The traveller ages 8 years and the stay-at-home 15.',
   note:'Proper acceleration is the derivative of rapidity, exactly: <b>dφ/dτ = a(τ)</b>. That is the same fact as "rapidities add" from the velocity-addition stage, written differentially, and it is why "hold one g forever" is a coherent instruction while "gain 10 m/s every second forever" is not. Write your own a(τ) and the machinery does not change — the final speed is <b>tanh</b> of the area under whatever you write, so no engine and no amount of patience reaches c. Two things worth noticing in the last card. The proper time is computed twice: once as the variable the equations were integrated in, and once as the <b>length of the curve</b> that produced, added up chord by chord — and the second comes out <i>above</i> the first, always, because a chord is the straight route between two events and in this geometry the straight route is the longest. And the <b>Rindler horizon</b>: while the engine is on, no signal from more than 1/a astern ever catches up. A horizon built out of nothing but motion, in flat spacetime, with no mass anywhere.'},
  {n:'One g forever — the galaxy in twenty years', ex:'τ ≈ (2c/a)·ln(aD/c²)', stage:'rlRocket',
   opts:{dest:'galactic'},
   out:'Hold a comfortable 1 g, turn over halfway, and arrive at rest. The worldline is a hyperbola asymptotic to the light cone. The galactic centre, 26 000 light-years away, costs about twenty years of your life — and 26 000 years of everyone else\'s.',
   note:'Proper time grows only <b>logarithmically</b> with distance, because γ climbs exponentially in the traveller\'s own clock, so Andromeda is 29 years away and the edge of the observable universe is about 45. The bill is at the other end: the home clock tracks the light-travel time, linearly, and always will. Note the <b>Rindler horizon</b> trailing about a light-year behind the ship. While the acceleration is held, no signal from beyond it ever catches up — acceleration alone builds a horizon out of nothing but motion. That is not a curiosity; it is the closest thing in flat spacetime to a black hole horizon, and an accelerating detector really does register a thermal bath (the <b>Unruh effect</b>) where an inertial one finds vacuum.'},
  {n:'Any thrust programme you like — and the ceiling it cannot cross', ex:'β = tanh(∫a dτ), for every a you can write', stage:'rlRocket',
   opts:{rmode:'prog', romkey:'pulse'},
   out:'One Gaussian burn, then coast. The upper plot shows the engine and the rapidity it delivers — the second is the area under the first. The lower one shows what that buys: a speed that saturates against c, and a home clock running away.',
   note:'Try each preset and watch the same relationship. A <b>rising thrust</b> (0.3τ) gives a rapidity that climbs quadratically and a speed that piles up against the barrier without touching it. A <b>runaway</b> a(τ) = 1/(3.01 − τ) has a pole just past the end of the run; push τ₁ past 3.01 and the panel refuses, because reaching infinite rapidity in finite proper time is not a motion — it is the one thing this engine will not integrate, and it says so rather than returning a number. Note what the integration never does: divide by 1 − β². Working in rapidity removes every singular denominator, which is why a programme that reaches 0.999999999c costs exactly as much arithmetic as one that dawdles. The energetics is the part relativity does not excuse — reaching γ = 1000 means converting hundreds of times the ship\'s mass at perfect efficiency — but the kinematics is entirely permissive, and this is what it permits.'},
]},
{ g:'Momentum, energy & mass', items:[
  {n:'E² = (pc)² + (mc²)² — the mass shell', ex:'and cosmic-ray muons, measured', stage:'rlDyn',
   opts:{p:'proton', beta:0.8, L:15000},
   out:'γ is a wall, not a slope: the energy needed diverges as β → 1. The middle plot is the hyperbola every free particle of that mass is confined to — a boost slides it along the curve and never off it. The right-hand plot is the experiment that settles time dilation.',
   note:'<b>E = mc²</b> is the least interesting equation here. The content is <b>E² − (pc)² = (mc²)²</b>, which says four-momentum has a fixed length and <i>mass is that length</i> — not a quantity of stuff, and not something that grows with speed. Watch the residual in the readout stay at zero while E runs away. "Relativistic mass" was abandoned for a good reason: inertia is <b>γ³m</b> along the motion and only <b>γm</b> across it, so no single number could be "the mass". The muon panel is the cleanest confirmation anyone has: at γ = 20 a third of the beam crosses 15 km of atmosphere, where without dilation essentially none would — a discrepancy of nine orders of magnitude. In the muon\'s own frame nothing lives any longer; the atmosphere is merely 750 m thick. Two descriptions, one count at the bottom.'},
  {n:'Write a collision, and watch what is conserved and what is not', ex:'E and p are; the sum of the masses is not', stage:'rlDyn',
   opts:{dymode:'collide', xkey:'clay'},
   out:'List the particles coming in and going out — a mass and a β each — and the panel adds their four-momenta tip to tail. Two lumps of clay at 0.6c meet head-on and stop dead, and the single lump left behind has mass <b>2.5</b>, not 2.',
   note:'That half a unit is the kinetic energy, and it did not go anywhere: it <i>is</i> the extra mass. E = mc² as an arithmetic identity rather than a slogan — and it is where almost all of a proton\'s mass comes from, since its quarks are nearly massless and the rest is the energy binding them. Three things are worth separating. <b>Energy and momentum are conserved</b>; the <b>invariant mass of the whole system</b> is conserved and is the same in every frame (the panel boosts the lot by an arbitrary 0.63 and recomputes it); and the <b>sum of the individual masses</b> is not conserved at all. Comparing that last one across the arrow is the commonest way to get this wrong. Try the <b>photons</b> preset, where two massless particles make a system of invariant mass 2, and the <b>fixed target</b> one, where a 0.99c beam on a stationary target has only 4.02 of invariant mass against 14.2 for the same two collided head-on. That factor is the entire reason colliders exist. And the <b>elastic</b> preset is deliberately unbalanced: the panel says so, and by how much, because an arrow between two lists is not a physical process.'},
  {n:'Doppler, aberration and the headlight effect', ex:'the sky rearranges itself', stage:'rlDopp',
   opts:{beta:0.7},
   out:'The stars pile up ahead of you and blueshift; behind you they thin out and redden. An isotropic emitter\'s light is beamed into a cone of half-angle about 1/γ and brightened by δ⁴ inside it.',
   note:'The transverse row in the readout is the one with no classical counterpart at all: a source at its closest approach, with no line-of-sight motion, is <i>still</i> redshifted — by exactly 1/γ. That is time dilation observed directly, and Ives and Stilwell measured it on a hydrogen ion beam in 1938. Beaming explains why relativistic jets so often appear one-sided: two identical jets, one towards us and one away, differ in brightness by <b>(δ_forward/δ_back)⁴</b>, which at β = 0.99 is a factor of millions. And the largest Doppler shift anyone has measured is our own: the microwave background is one part in a thousand hotter in one direction than the other, a 3.36 mK dipole that is nothing but this stage applied to the Local Group\'s 370 km/s drift.'},
  {n:'Point the source anywhere — and find the angle with no shift at all', ex:'it is not 90°, and that is the whole point', stage:'rlDopp',
   opts:{smode:'shift', skey:'transverse'},
   out:'δ = 1/[γ(1 − β cos θ)], swept over every angle in your frame. At 0° the classical blueshift is amplified; at 180° the redshift is; and at <b>90°</b>, where there is no line-of-sight motion whatever, the signal is <i>still</i> redshifted — by exactly 1/γ.',
   note:'The transverse case is the one with no classical counterpart at all. A Newtonian calculation gives no shift at 90°, because nothing is approaching or receding; relativity gives 1/γ, and it is time dilation observed directly. Ives and Stilwell measured it on a hydrogen ion beam in 1938 and it remains the cleanest test there is. Now look at the marked angle on the left where δ = 1 — where the signal is genuinely <b>unshifted</b>. It is <i>not</i> 90°: it sits forward of it, at cos θ = (1 − 1/γ)/β, because the time-dilation redshift has to be cancelled by a real approach before the net shift can reach zero. The classical null and the relativistic null are different angles, and the gap between them is the effect. The lower plot is δ⁴, which is four separate facts multiplied: each photon is shifted, they arrive more often, and the solid angle they occupy contracts by δ² — which is why two identical jets, one towards us and one away, can differ in brightness by a factor of millions.'},
]},
{ g:'Electromagnetism is relativity', items:[
  {n:'Boosting the field — six numbers, one object', ex:'E·B and E² − c²B² never move', stage:'rlEB',
   opts:{fkey:'pureE', beta:0.6},
   out:'Set any field on the left and watch what a moving observer measures on the right. Components along the boost are untouched; perpendicular ones mix. The lower plot sweeps every boost: |E| and |B| move, and the two invariants are dead flat.',
   note:'E and B are not two fields that happen to be coupled. They are six components of one antisymmetric tensor, and a boost rotates them into each other exactly as a spatial rotation rotates x into y. The two invariants decide what kind of field you actually have, and no observer can argue: if <b>E·B = 0</b> and <b>E² > c²B²</b> there is a frame with <i>no magnetic field at all</i>, and the magnetism was never anything but the electric field of these charges seen from a moving frame. Try the presets. A light wave has both invariants zero, which is why no frame can make it purely electric, purely magnetic, or stationary — the same obstruction that ends the "chasing a light beam" stage.'},
  {n:'Six numbers you choose, and the frame they name', ex:'the classification, visited rather than asserted', stage:'rlEB',
   opts:{fkey:'skew', beta:0.4},
   out:'Type any E and any B. The panel reads the two invariants, classifies the field from them, and then <b>goes to the frame that classification promises</b> and measures what is left there. With E·B ≠ 0 neither field can be removed — and the frame that makes them parallel is found anyway.',
   note:'"There is a frame in which the magnetic field vanishes" is a claim about a <b>frame</b>, and a stage that prints it without visiting one has asserted rather than measured. This one boosts by the drift velocity and reports what survives, against the size of the field it started from — a residual that is meant to be zero cannot be scaled against itself. Two things worth trying. Set <b>E·B ≠ 0</b> (the preset here) and no boost removes either field, but one still makes them parallel: that frame solves <b>v/(1+v²) = |E×B|/(E²+B²)</b>, and the shipped E×B drift formula was the wrong root for it until this stage went and looked. Then set the <b>nearly null</b> preset, where E² only just beats B²: the frame exists, and it is moving at 0.98c. Push it to exactly null and it recedes to <i>c</i> — which is why light is light for everyone. The card also transforms the field twice, by the six component formulas and by <b>ΛFΛᵀ</b> with Λ pointing wherever your field points; nothing in this wing had checked that off the x axis.'},
  {n:'E becomes B: a charge in motion', ex:'the exact boosted Coulomb field', stage:'relBoost',
   opts:{beta:0.7},
   out:'In the charge\'s own frame: pure Coulomb, B = 0. In the lab, the same charge shows E compressed into the transverse plane (E⊥ × γ, E∥ ÷ γ²) and a magnetic field B = β×E circling the motion. The probe verifies that E² − B² is identical in both frames.',
   note:'Not a Lorentz-contracted Coulomb field — a Coulomb field whose <i>source</i> is contracted, which comes out differently and is drawn here exactly. Push β towards 0.99 and the field flattens into a pancake perpendicular to the motion. This is why an ultrarelativistic bunch at an accelerator arrives with its field squashed into a travelling wall, why that wall looks like a pulse of radiation to anything it passes (the Weizsäcker–Williams picture), and why synchrotron light is emitted in a forward-beamed sliver. Gauss\'s law still holds exactly: the total flux is 4πq at any speed, and the next demo goes and integrates it rather than saying so.'},
  {n:'Charges you place, and a flux that does not care how fast they move', ex:'∮E·dA = 4πq, integrated in both frames', stage:'relBoost',
   opts:{mode:'gauss', qkey:'ultra'},
   out:'Type any set of charges, at any speeds, and set a sphere around them. The panel integrates <b>∮E·dA</b> over it — once in the lab, where the field of a fast charge is a pancake γ³ times stronger across the motion than along it, and once over the same events in the charges\' own frame, where the surface is an <b>ellipsoid</b> and the field is plain Coulomb.',
   note:'The spikes drawn around the circle are E·n̂ itself, so you can see what is being added up: at γ = 7 the integrand varies by a factor of 350 around one sphere, and the total is unchanged to twelve digits. That is <b>charge invariance</b>, and it is the one conserved quantity that survives a boost untouched — energy, momentum, length and time all transform, and charge does not. Experimentally it is good to about a part in 10²¹, which is why a wire carrying a current is electrically neutral to the same precision even though its two charge populations have completely different length contractions applied to them. Two other things to try: the <b>dipole</b>, where the enclosed charge is zero so the total is a cancellation printed against ∮|E|dA — which is emphatically not zero; and the charge <b>outside</b> the sphere, felt everywhere on the surface and contributing nothing whatever to the total. That second half of Gauss\'s law is the half people forget.'},
  {n:'Where magnetism comes from — the wire', ex:'the same force, computed twice', stage:'rlWire',
   opts:{mode:'cartoon', bd:0.5, bv:0.6},
   out:'A neutral wire and a charge moving beside it. In the lab the force is purely magnetic. In the charge\'s frame there is no magnetic force at all — but the two lattices contract by different amounts, the wire carries net charge, and the force is purely electrostatic. The two answers agree to the last digit.',
   note:'This is the demonstration the wing is built around, and it is the argument Einstein opens the 1905 paper with. Move the magnet or move the conductor, and classical electrodynamics tells two entirely unrelated stories — an induced electric field in one case, a Lorentz force in the other — which happen to agree every single time. That coincidence, he wrote, "does not appear to be inherent in the phenomena". It is not a coincidence: they are the same field. Switch to <b>real speeds</b> and look at the size of the imbalance: electrons drift through household wiring at about a tenth of a millimetre per second, and the charge asymmetry that carries the entire magnetic force is a part in 10¹⁷ of either lattice. It survives only because the number of charges is correspondingly vast. Every magnet on your fridge is a relativistic effect, at a hundred-millionth of the speed of light.'},
  {n:'A wire you build yourself — and neutrality as a measurement', ex:'three species, two frames, one force', stage:'rlWire',
   opts:{mode:'sheet', wkeyw:'twoCarrier'},
   out:'Write the carrier species: a lab density and a drift speed each. The panel adds them up, reports whether the wire is neutral rather than assuming it, and computes the force on a passing charge in both frames — magnetic in the lab, electrostatic in the charge\'s own frame, and the same number.',
   note:'The textbook wire has exactly two species and is chosen to be neutral, and that choice quietly does a lot of the work. Here it is a measurement: type densities that do not cancel and the wire is <b>charged</b> in the lab, so both an electric and a magnetic force act — and the frames still agree, which is the point. Neutrality was never what made the argument work. Then load <b>realistic</b>, where the electrons drift at 3×10⁻¹³c, and look at the last card. The species-by-species sum — which is exactly what the argument says in words — has <b>no digits left</b>: the imbalance carrying the entire magnetic force is a part in 10¹⁷ of either density, and subtracting two such numbers in floating point returns noise. The closed form γ(v′) = γ(v)γ(βₜ)(1 − vβₜ) collapses the difference before anything cancels, and it is the only way to compute the effect at a speed anyone actually uses. Every motor on Earth runs on a number that cannot be obtained by doing the obvious thing.'},
  {n:'The field tensor — four equations become two', ex:"F′ = ΛFΛᵀ, done both ways", stage:'rlTensor',
   opts:{tkey:'general', beta:0.7},
   out:'The boost is a matrix conjugation and nothing more. The readout transforms the field by the vector formulas and by ΛFΛᵀ separately and reports the difference, which is zero.',
   note:'Antisymmetry is why there are six components rather than sixteen, and it is the deepest available answer to "why two fields, with three components each?" — a rank-2 antisymmetric tensor in four dimensions has exactly <b>4·3/2 = 6</b>. In this notation Maxwell\'s four equations become <b>∂_μF^μν = μ₀J^ν</b> and <b>∂_[λF_μν] = 0</b>, and their form is manifestly the same in every frame. This is why Maxwell needed no repair when relativity arrived: his equations were <i>already</i> relativistic, and it was Newton\'s mechanics that had to change. Lorentz found the transformation a decade early, as a curiosity of electrodynamics; it took Einstein to recognise it as a property of space and time rather than of the ether.'},
  {n:'Sixteen numbers you type, and what they turn out to be', ex:'antisymmetry measured, not imposed', stage:'rlTensor',
   opts:{tkey:'broken', beta:0.5},
   out:'Type any 4×4. The panel measures the worst <b>|F<sup>μν</sup> + F<sup>νμ</sup>|</b> against the largest entry, reads E and B off the components, and rebuilds both invariants from the double contractions — which must agree with E·B and E² − B² computed from the vectors.',
   note:'This preset is deliberately <b>not</b> antisymmetric, and the panel says so and says where. That matters more than it looks: silently deleting the symmetric part would be teaching the opposite of the lesson, because the whole content of writing the field this way is that antisymmetry is <i>forced</i> — it is what leaves exactly six components, and six is how many electromagnetism has. Fix the bottom-right block to <b>−1</b> and watch it become a field. Then compare the two ways the invariants are computed: <b>F<sub>μν</sub>F<sup>μν</sup></b> and <b>F<sub>μν</sub>F̃<sup>μν</sup></b> are scalars built from the sixteen components with no mention of E or B, while E·B and E² − B² are built from the vectors with no mention of the tensor. Same two numbers. That coincidence is the reason this notation exists.'}
]},
{ g:'Curved spacetime', items:[
  {n:'The Schwarzschild metric — and why GPS works', ex:'ds² = −(1−rs/r)c²dt² + dr²/(1−rs/r) + r²dΩ²', stage:'rlMetric',
   opts:{key:'schwarzschild', body:'sun', rr:6, p1:20, p2:40},
   out:'Two coefficients do all the work, and the panel measures rather than asserts that they are reciprocals. Everything else is located: the horizon is the sign change of the first, at r = 2 in units of GM/c², which is 2GM/c² to the last bit. The funnel on the left is a true embedding — distances across the drawn surface are the real ones.',
   note:'Schwarzschild found this within weeks of Einstein publishing the field equations, while serving on the Russian front, and died of an illness contracted there a few months later. It contains every classical test and a black hole nobody wanted for another forty years. Two things worth noticing. First, the rubber-sheet picture — which is what the paraboloid <i>is</i>, done properly — contains no time at all, and almost all of everyday gravity is the <b>time</b> part of the metric. An apple falls because worldlines that linger lower down accumulate more proper time, not because space is dented. Second, the proper radial distance really is larger than the coordinate difference: there is more room down there than there is space to put it in. The GPS card computes the 38 μs/day drift from the metric and the orbit; the satellites\' oscillators are detuned before launch to compensate.'},
  {n:"Mercury's perihelion — the 43 arcseconds", ex:'Δφ = 2∫(L/r²)√(AB/(E²−V²))dr − 2π', stage:'rlOrbit',
   opts:{key:'schwarzschild', rp:20, ecc:0.35, orbits:5},
   out:'The orbit is a geodesic of whatever is in the two boxes, and the advance between pericentres is measured from it twice — once by locating them along the integrated track, once by an integral with no geodesic in it. They agree to about ten figures. The first-order formula 6πGM/c²a(1−e²) is drawn beside them as a third line, and at this pericentre it is 21% low: it is an expansion, and you are outside it.',
   note:'Le Verrier found the 43″ discrepancy in 1859 and proposed a new planet, Vulcan, to account for it; people reported seeing it. Einstein computed this number in November 1915 and wrote that it gave him heart palpitations — it is the only classical test that was a <i>retrodiction</i>, with the measurement already on the table and no free parameters left to tune. The mechanism is worth understanding: an inverse-square force is one of only two central forces whose bound orbits close at all, and the closure is fragile. General relativity adds a <b>1/r⁴</b> term, which grows faster than the centrifugal barrier and eventually beats it entirely — which is where the innermost stable circular orbit at 3rs comes from, a feature Newton simply does not have, and the reason accretion discs have a bright inner edge. Bring the pericentre in to watch the ellipse become a rosette, and note that the formula and the measurement part company on the way.'},
  {n:'Delete the curvature of space, and lose exactly a third', ex:'A = 1 − 2GM/c²r,  B = 1', stage:'rlOrbit',
   opts:{key:'newton', rp:400, ecc:0.7, orbits:5},
   out:'The same orbit in the metric Newton would have written — curved time, flat space. It still precesses, but the measured advance is 0.671 of the first-order Schwarzschild value, and it converges on exactly 2/3 as the orbit widens. Switch back to Schwarzschild without touching the sliders and the ratio jumps to 1.007.',
   note:'This is the perihelion twin of the famous factor of two in light bending, and it is the cleanest way to answer the thing everyone is told — that gravity is curved <i>time</i>. That is most of the truth for a slow planet and none of it for a photon. Keeping only g<sub>tt</sub> reproduces Newtonian gravity almost perfectly, bends light by <b>half</b> the observed angle, and advances a perihelion by <b>two-thirds</b> of the observed amount. The missing shares are the same thing in both cases: the spatial curvature g<sub>rr</sub> you have just deleted, which contributes nothing to a slow orbit\'s energy and a great deal to the geometry of the path. Eddington measured the light half in 1919; Le Verrier had unknowingly measured the orbital third sixty years earlier. Note that the horizon, the photon sphere and the ISCO do not move — they depend on A alone — so a metric can agree with Schwarzschild on every circular orbit and still be wrong about every eccentric one.'},
  {n:'Bending light — 1.75 arcseconds, and lensing', ex:'Δθ = 4GM/c²b, exactly twice Newton', stage:'rlLens',
   opts:{key:'schwarzschild', body:'sun', b:12},
   out:'Every ray here is integrated from the null geodesic equation of whatever is in the two boxes, and the deflection is then measured a second time by a quadrature with no geodesic in it. At the solar limb the answer is 1.7512″, computed rather than quoted, and the panel prints its distance from the first-order 4GM/c²b — which is not noise but the second-order term, and falls like 1/b.',
   note:'Eddington and Dyson photographed the eclipse of 29 May 1919 from Príncipe and Sobral, the result was announced that November, and Einstein was on the front page of the London <i>Times</i> the next morning. The factor of two is the whole test: Newtonian gravity acting on corpuscles gives half, and only curved <i>space</i> supplies the rest. The modern name for the ratio is the post-Newtonian <b>γ</b>, and the panel measures it by running one quadrature twice over the same A — once with your B and once with B = 1 — so it is a measurement and not a claim; Cassini has γ − 1 = (2.1 ± 2.3)×10⁻⁵. Bring b down towards <b>b_c = 3√3 GM/c²</b> and the small-angle expansion fails honestly: rays wind several times round before escaping, and the lower-right panel measures how many radians each decade of approach buys against a prediction made from A, B and W″ at the photon sphere alone. Inside b_c nothing comes out at all, and that circle is the shadow the Event Horizon Telescope photographed — larger than the horizon, and the reason it is larger is in the readout.'},
  {n:'Delete the curvature of space, and lose exactly half', ex:'A = 1 − 2GM/c²r,  B = 1', stage:'rlLens',
   opts:{key:'newton', body:'sun', b:12},
   out:'The same rays in the metric Newton would have written — curved time, flat space. The photon sphere is still at r = 3, the critical impact parameter is still 3√3, and every circular orbit is exactly where it was, because all three depend on A alone. The deflection is halved, and the measured γ comes out 0.',
   note:'This is the light half of the wing\'s recurring subtraction, and it is the largest of the three: flattening g<sub>rr</sub> costs a perihelion advance one third of its size, costs the frozen star entirely, and costs light <b>half</b> its bend. Watch what does <i>not</i> move — this metric agrees with Schwarzschild about every clock rate, every redshift, the horizon, the photon sphere and the capture radius. What it disagrees about is the geometry of a <i>path</i>, and a photon is nothing but path. Look at the winding panel too: the Lyapunov exponent changes from 1 to √3, so a near-critical ray here gains only 1.329 radians per decade instead of 2.303. The winding rate carries √(A·B), and there is no way to see that from any clock.'},
  {n:'Falling in — finite for you, forever for everyone else', ex:'proper time ends; coordinate time does not', stage:'rlHole',
   opts:{key:'schwarzschild', body:'hole', r0:20, dec:2},
   out:'Both clocks are quadratures of the two boxes, in variables chosen to remove the singularity at each end. The faller\'s own clock reaches the horizon in a few seconds and nothing local marks the crossing; the coordinate clock runs off the top of the frame. The lower-right panel is the honest version of that arrow — it measures the coordinate time added by each successive halving of the remaining gap, and the bars are level.',
   note:'The horizon is not a wall and there is no local experiment that detects it; it is a one-way surface in the causal structure. What is genuinely strange is the signalling: the redshift falls off as a straight line on the log plot, so the image does not stop — it dims to nothing. Two numbers deserve attention. The mean density inside a horizon falls as <b>1/M²</b>, so a few-billion-solar-mass hole is less dense than water and nothing about a horizon requires crushing matter, only enough mass inside a radius. And the tidal force at the horizon goes as <b>c⁶/G²M²</b>, which means bigger holes are <i>gentler</i>: change the body picker to Sgr A* without touching anything else — the geometry in units of GM/c² is identical and only the exchange rate into metres has moved — and watch the verdict flip from fatal to survivable. The rate of the freezing is worth a second look too. E cancels out of it entirely, so every infaller freezes at the same exponential rate whatever height they were dropped from: it is a property of the horizon, equal to 1/2κ with κ the surface gravity — the same κ that sets the hole\'s Hawking temperature.'},
  {n:'Delete the curvature of space, and the frozen star goes with it', ex:'A = 1 − 2GM/c²r,  B = 1', stage:'rlHole',
   opts:{key:'newton', body:'hole', r0:20, dec:2},
   out:'The same fall in the metric Newton would have written — curved time, flat space. The horizon is still at r = 2, every clock rate is unchanged, and the proper time to the horizon barely moves. But the bars on the lower right collapse by a factor of 1/√2 each halving instead of staying level, their sum converges, and the coordinate time to the horizon is <b>finite</b>. Nothing freezes.',
   note:'This is the third time in the wing that flattening <b>g<sub>rr</sub></b> costs something, and it is the largest of the three. Keeping only the time coefficient reproduces Newtonian gravity almost perfectly, bends light by <b>half</b> the observed angle, advances a perihelion by <b>two-thirds</b>, and now loses the frozen star <i>entirely</i>. The mechanism is one factor: the coordinate-time integrand carries √(A·B), which is 1 for every vacuum solution and gives a simple pole at the horizon — but B = 1 makes A·B vanish there, the pole softens to an integrable inverse square root, and the integral converges. Since A is untouched, no argument about gravitational time dilation can account for the difference; the freezing was never the time part. Then look at the tidal row, which reports that the stretch at r = 2 is <b>unbounded</b>. That is not a numerical failure: with A·B → 0 the curvature genuinely diverges as 1/(r − r_h)², so this metric has a naked curvature singularity exactly where Schwarzschild has a perfectly smooth horizon. It agrees with Schwarzschild about every clock rate, every circular orbit, the photon sphere and the ISCO, and it is a completely different object.'},
  {n:'Type a metric — and see what can still be located in it', ex:'A(r), B(r) → horizons, photon sphere, ISCO, geodesics', stage:'rlMetric',
   opts:{key:'desitter', body:'hole', rr:8, p1:8, p2:12},
   out:'Nothing in this panel is read off a formula for Schwarzschild. The horizons are the sign changes of A, found by bisection; the photon sphere is a root of A′r = 2A; the ISCO is where the circular-orbit L² turns round; and the orbit is integrated from the Christoffel symbols of whatever is in the two boxes. E and L are never imposed on it, so their drift is a measurement.',
   note:'This metric is a black hole sitting in an expanding universe, and it is chosen because it breaks four assumptions the wing had quietly been making. It has <b>two</b> horizons rather than one, and they are of completely different kinds — a black-hole horizon just outside r = 2 and a <b>cosmological</b> one out at r ≈ 99, past which expansion carries everything away for good. The region a static observer can occupy is the band <i>between</i> them, which is the one place "outside the outermost horizon" is not; a scan written the obvious way returns nothing at all here, and that mistake is exactly what the engine\'s own test suite made on its first run. The photon sphere is at <b>exactly 3</b> whatever Λ is, because the Λ terms cancel out of A′r = 2A — worth checking against the panel. And there is an <b>outermost</b> stable circular orbit as well as an innermost one: far enough out the cosmological term wins and orbits stop being stable again, so "the outermost place where L² stops changing" is the wrong way to find an ISCO. Try the other presets: the charged hole has two horizons that merge and then vanish as the charge grows, past which the singularity is naked; "only time curved" keeps A and flattens B, which leaves every circular orbit exactly where it was — they depend on A alone — while halving the light deflection; and Minkowski is the control that has to report that nothing happens.'},
  {n:'Type a mass profile — and watch a halo bend every ray the same', ex:'M(r) → A = 1 − 2M/r,  B = 1/A', stage:'rlLens',
   opts:{key:'mass', srcM:'r/12', body:'sun', b:20},
   out:'A mass profile is a metric once you say what holds it up, and the panel names the model it uses: the family with A·B = 1, which contains Schwarzschild (M = 1), the charged hole (M = 1 − Q²/2r) and the cosmological constant (M = 1 + Λr³/6) as three different M(r). This one is a halo whose mass grows in proportion to r, and the measured log–log slope of the deflection against b is <b>zero</b>: every ray is bent by the same angle, whatever its impact parameter.',
   note:'Two profiles are worth typing. <b>min(1, (r/8)^3)</b> is a uniform sphere of radius 8, and outside it the deflection is identical to a point mass of the same total <i>to the last bit</i> — that is Birkhoff\'s theorem, measured rather than cited, and the panel will show you the difference is exactly zero. Bring b inside 9.24 and the two part company, because now the ray is passing through the mass. <b>r/12</b> is the halo above: A is the constant 1 − 1/6, which makes the spacetime a cone, and the deflection is exactly π(1/√(1−2k) − 1) at every b — a closed form the panel checks itself against. Notice what that costs: with A constant there is no gravitational time dilation anywhere, so the "time only" row is <b>zero</b> and the entire bend is spatial curvature. A point mass falls off as 1/b and a halo does not fall off at all, which is why the arcs a galaxy cluster produces are the wrong size for its visible matter, and why lensing is the principal evidence for extended dark-matter haloes rather than concentrated ones.'}
]},
{ g:'Ripples in spacetime', items:[
  {n:'Gravitational waves — GW150914, computed from its two masses', ex:'ȧ = −(64/5)m₁m₂M/a³, and everything follows', stage:'rlWave',
   opts:{key:'gw150914', inc:0, amp:0.30},
   out:'Nothing in this panel is a formula for GW150914. The inspiral is integrated from the quadrupole energy balance, which knows the two masses separately; the frequency at each sample is Kepler\'s; the sweep rate is differenced off that track; and the wave on the lower right is the two bodies\' own quadrupole moment, differentiated twice numerically. The chirp mass then comes back out of the waveform and agrees with (m₁m₂)^(3/5)/(m₁+m₂)^(1/5) to eight figures.',
   note:'Einstein derived these in 1916, then in 1936 submitted a paper arguing they did not exist (a referee caught the error; Einstein, unused to peer review, withdrew it in a temper and published a corrected version elsewhere). There is no monopole radiation because mass is conserved and no dipole because momentum is conserved, so the leading term is the <b>quadrupole</b> — which is also why the wave arrives at <i>twice</i> the orbital frequency, a quadrupole repeating itself after half a turn. The panel counts the zero crossings rather than telling you. For about twenty milliseconds this event radiated three solar masses as pure spacetime strain, at a peak power of roughly <b>3.6×10⁴⁹ W</b>: more than all the light of every star in the observable universe, and none of it visible. What reached LIGO was a length change of 10⁻¹⁸ m across a 4 km arm — a ten-thousandth of a proton.'},
  {n:'Weighing a binary you have never seen — the chirp mass', ex:'Mc = [(5/96)π^(−8/3)ḟ f^(−11/3)]^(3/5)', stage:'rlWave',
   opts:{key:'custom', m1:60, m2:15, aKm:1400, dMpc:100, inc:0, amp:0.30},
   out:'A 4:1 pair, typed rather than catalogued. The dashed curve is a completely different binary — two equal masses of 28.7 M☉ — and it lies exactly on top of the solid one, because the two have the same chirp mass. They differ only in where they stop: the two marked endpoints are their innermost stable orbits, which the total mass sets. That is why the second card measures a chirp mass to eight figures off the waveform and why the component masses are so much harder.',
   note:'This is how every mass in the GWTC catalogues was obtained, and the logic is worth following. The sweep rate depends on the two masses only through <b>Mc = (m₁m₂)<sup>3/5</sup>/(m₁+m₂)<sup>1/5</sup></b>, so measuring f and ḟ gives that one number with no distance, no orientation and no source model needed. The amplitude then gives the distance — which is why these are called <i>standard sirens</i> and why GW170817 produced an independent measurement of the Hubble constant. What separates 60 + 15 from 28.7 + 28.7 is the total mass, and that only enters at the <b>end</b>: the two tracks part company at the innermost stable circular orbit, which is 6GM/c² for each. Slide the masses apart and watch the dashed twin stay put while the ISCO marker moves.'},
  {n:'Edge on, and the polarisation no binary can make', ex:'h₊ : h× = 1+cos²ι : 2cos ι', stage:'rlWave',
   opts:{key:'gw170817', inc:90, amp:0.34},
   out:'Face-on, the two polarisations are equal and a quarter-cycle apart, so the ring turns steadily — the wave is circularly polarised. Tilt to 90° and h× vanishes exactly while h₊ falls to half: the ring now stretches along one axis and back. Both amplitudes are measured off the numerically differentiated quadrupole and printed beside the closed-form pattern.',
   note:'A binary cannot radiate a pure <b>×</b> polarisation, and the reason is geometry rather than physics: the cross pattern <i>is</i> the plus pattern rotated by 45°, so a source producing one produces the other with the axes turned. What inclination actually changes is the ratio of the two and the total amplitude, and that is how a detector network infers the orientation of a system it cannot resolve. GW170817 was known to be nearly face-on before anyone looked, which is part of why its gamma-ray burst was seen at all. Note the ratio the panel prints: at 60° the wave is still 62% of its face-on strength, so gravitational-wave detectors are far less directional than telescopes — they hear most of the sky at once, and that is both their weakness and the reason they need three of them to locate anything.'},
  {n:'The same formulas on a binary pulsar — twenty decades of inspiral', ex:'from 1.95 million km to 25 km', stage:'rlWave',
   opts:{key:'psr1913', inc:60, amp:0.28},
   out:'Hulse–Taylor\'s two neutron stars, treated as a circular binary. The chirp axis now spans twenty decades of time rather than a fifth of a second, the frequency is 7×10⁻⁵ Hz, and the strain at 5.25 kpc is 10⁻²³ — a thousand times too small and a million times too slow for LIGO. The cycle count still comes out — 5.9×10¹² wave cycles, integrated as a quadrature and agreeing with ∫f dt to nine figures.',
   note:'This system is the reason anybody believed in gravitational waves before 2015, and its orbit is measurably shrinking — but not for the reason drawn here. Its eccentricity is <b>0.617</b>, which multiplies the radiated power by 11.86 and is the whole content of the next experiment. Two things are worth noticing even in this circular approximation. The <b>time to merger</b> is a few hundred million years, so this is a binary that will one day do what GW150914 did. And the cycle count is worth a look — <b>5.9×10¹²</b> wave cycles remain, and the panel gets that by integrating <b>φ̇ = ω(a)</b> along the same track rather than by resolving 3×10¹¹ individual orbits, which no step could. The accumulated phase is a quadrature of a smooth function, so the count agrees with the closed-form ∫f dt to nine figures; an earlier version of this stage bounded the step by the orbital period instead, ran into its own step limit, and printed a count 571 cycles short.'},
  {n:'The Sun and the Earth — two hundred watts', ex:'L = (32/5)m₁²m₂²M/a⁵, in SI', stage:'rlWave',
   opts:{key:'sunearth', inc:30, amp:0.22},
   out:'The control. The Earth\'s orbit radiates about 196 watts of gravitational waves — three light bulbs — and shrinks by roughly a proton\'s width every three centuries. The time to merger comes out at 10²³ years, which is 10¹³ times the age of the universe.',
   note:'A laboratory that cannot report "nothing measurable happens" cannot be trusted when it reports that something did, and this is the row that earns the rest. The same three formulas that give GW150914 its 3.6×10⁴⁹ watts give the Earth 196, and the difference is entirely in <b>m₁²m₂²M/a⁵</b>: compactness is everything, and a quantity that falls as the fifth power of separation is why only objects the size of a city radiate detectably. Notice also the ceiling in the last card — <b>c⁵/G = 3.6×10⁵² W</b> is a luminosity built out of constants alone, and a merging binary reaches a few thousandths of it. There is nothing brighter in the universe, and it is invisible.'},
  {n:'The first evidence — an orbit paying for its own radiation', ex:'Ṗ = −(192π/5)(2πMc/P)^(5/3)F(e)', stage:'rlDecay',
   opts:{key:'psr1913'},
   out:'Hulse–Taylor\'s orbit is shrinking by three and a half metres a year, and its period is shortening by 76 microseconds a year. The panel computes that decay from the two masses and the orbit — the average power by integrating round the ellipse, then again in closed form, then through Kepler\'s third law — and prints it beside the number a fifty-year timing campaign measured. They agree to two parts in a thousand.',
   note:'This was the first evidence that gravitational waves exist and carry energy away, and it arrived twenty-two years before one was caught directly; Hulse and Taylor were given the Nobel prize for it in 1993. Two things are worth dwelling on. The first is that <b>the eccentricity is doing most of the work</b>: 0.617 multiplies the radiated power by <b>11.86</b>, so a circular calculation would be low by an order of magnitude and the confirmation would have looked like a refutation. The second is what is being compared — every other agreement in this wing is between two calculations, and this one is between a calculation and a telescope. The residual is dominated by how well the two masses are known, because Ṗ goes as <b>Mc<sup>5/3</sup></b>; the timing itself is far better than that.'},
  {n:'Where the radiation actually happens', ex:'P ∝ r⁻⁴(12v² − 11ṙ²) — a pulse, not a hum', stage:'rlDecay',
   opts:{key:'custom', m1:1.4, m2:1.4, pd:0.1, ecc:0.9},
   out:'Two neutron stars on a very eccentric orbit. The power at pericentre is <b>4.7×10⁷</b> times the power at apocentre and 153 times the orbit average, and the upper right panel shows the emission as a spike occupying a few per cent of the period. Peters\' factor F(0.9) is <b>1243</b>, and the panel measures it by integrating round the ellipse rather than by using the formula.',
   note:'This is why <b>(1−e²)<sup>−7/2</sup></b> appears in every one of these formulas, and it is worth seeing rather than being told. Radiated power goes as <b>r⁻⁴</b> times a velocity term, and at pericentre a body is both closest and fastest — so almost the entire output of an eccentric binary happens in the sliver of the orbit near closest approach. Drag the eccentricity to 0.95 and watch the peak leave the top of the frame. The consequence for astrophysics is large: eccentric binaries merge far faster than circular ones of the same size, which is how dynamically formed pairs in dense clusters reach the merger stage at all, and it is why the lower panel\'s two routes have to agree over the whole slider rather than at one point.'},
  {n:'The sharpest test there is — the double pulsar', ex:'observed ÷ predicted = 0.999963 ± 0.000063', stage:'rlDecay',
   opts:{key:'j0737'},
   out:'PSR J0737−3039 is the only known system where both neutron stars are visible as pulsars, so the mass ratio is a measurement rather than a fit. Its eccentricity is only 0.088, so F(e) is 1.05 and the enhancement barely matters — and sixteen years of timing put the observed decay within four parts in a hundred thousand of the prediction.',
   note:'This system has replaced Hulse–Taylor as the headline test of general relativity in a strong field, and the reason is worth understanding: seeing <i>both</i> pulsars gives the mass ratio directly from the two orbits, which removes the single largest source of uncertainty in the prediction. Kramer and colleagues reported the 2021 result after sixteen years of timing, and it also measures the Shapiro delay, geodetic precession and light bending in the same system. Compare its <b>F(e) = 1.05</b> with Hulse–Taylor\'s <b>11.86</b> and the difference in the two experiments becomes clear — one tests the quadrupole formula with the eccentric enhancement doing most of the work, the other tests it almost naked.'}
]}];
